1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
 Hi, and welcome to this course on MCP server security.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
 Before we dive into vulnerabilities and attacks,

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:13,000
 we need to understand what MCP is and why it exists.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,000
 MCP stands for Model Context Protocol.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:20,000
 It's a standard way to make external application

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,000
 provide context to LLM models.

7
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,000
 So what does that actually mean?

8
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:30,000
 Imagine you use an AI model like Claude or Chab GBT.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,000
 With this model, you can enable online search

10
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:36,000
 where the model can search the web for you

11
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:40,000
 or you can enable a tool that will allow the model

12
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:42,000
 to create an image for you.

13
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,000
 So those are like built-in capabilities.

14
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,000
 However, if you ask the model to sort your files on Google Drive

15
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:54,000
 or maybe summarize your email from Gmail

16
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:57,000
 or translate your private YouTube videos

17
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,000
 into a language like German

18
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
 or maybe access a content from your Contentful CMS solution,

19
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:07,000
 then in this case, the LLM model

20
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:11,000
 won't be able to do that out of the box.

21
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,000
 So why the LLM model cannot access your data?

22
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:17,000
 There are two main reasons for that.

23
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:21,000
 First, the LLM is not designed to access external systems

24
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:22,000
 directly.

25
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,000
 They are designed to generate text based on the user input

26
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:28,000
 that they receive.

27
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,000
 The second reason is the LLM are not aware

28
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,000
 of the context of your external systems.

29
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:37,000
 So they do not really know how to authenticate

30
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,000
 for example to your Google Drive

31
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:43,000
 or how to use Contentful Management Authorization

32
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:48,000
 token to access your website content that is hosted on Contentful.

33
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,000
 Oh, MCP comes to solve this problem.

34
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:54,000
 MCP is the universal connector.

35
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:59,000
 So MCP gives you a standard way to build a server

36
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:04,000
 that can connect the LLM model to an external applications

37
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:07,000
 and APIs just like USB-C.

38
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,000
 It lets you connect any device to your computer.

39
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:14,000
 So MCP lets you connect any data source

40
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:17,000
 like Google Drive or maybe YouTube

41
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:21,000
 or maybe Contentful to your AI model.

42
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,000
 Once it's connected, you can ask the LLM

43
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:26,000
 to interact with your data

44
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:30,000
 and you can simply say list my files on Google Drive

45
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:33,000
 and then maybe sort them by size.

46
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:35,000
 Let's walk through a real example.

47
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:40,000
 When a user asks list or get all my files on Google Drive

48
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:42,000
 and then maybe sort them by size.

49
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:45,000
 Here's what's going to happen behind the scenes.

50
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:50,000
 The LLM is going to check if there is an MCP server integration

51
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:53,000
 that can handle this specific request.

52
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:57,000
 It finds out that Google Drive MCP server

53
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:01,000
 and then it sends the request to the MCP server.

54
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:04,000
 The MCP server is going to authenticate

55
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:09,000
 against Google using your authorization token.

56
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:13,000
 After that, the MCP is able to fetch file names

57
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:17,000
 and metadata through Google API.

58
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:21,000
 Once it gets that, it will return this data back

59
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:23,000
 to the LLM model.

60
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,000
 Now at this stage, the LLM can now generate a response

61
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:32,000
 with your actual files sorted by size back to the user.

62
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:36,000
 The magic is that the LLM model itself

63
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,000
 it never touches the Google Drive.

64
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:44,000
 The MCP is the one that handles all the complex authentication

65
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:46,000
 and API calls.

66
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:50,000
 Get the data from Google Drive, send it back to the LLM model

67
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,000
 and once the LLM model has the data like files metadata,

68
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,000
 it can sort them and send them back to the user

69
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:59,000
 based on the user request.

70
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:02,000
 Now let's take a business use case

71
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:05,000
 to help you understand how powerful the MCP server is.

72
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:09,000
 Imagine you work for a company that has a website

73
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:13,000
 and they host their website content on a CMS solution

74
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:15,000
 like Contentful.

75
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:18,000
 And you have a task to translate the website content

76
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:21,000
 into a new language, let's say, German.

77
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:24,000
 Typically what you have to do, first,

78
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:28,000
 you need to get the latest blog post using Contentful API

79
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:32,000
 or maybe manually through their user interface.

80
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:37,000
 Once you get the content, then you need to go to the AI model

81
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:39,000
 for the translation.

82
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:41,000
 If you are satisfied with the translation,

83
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:46,000
 you need to go back to Contentful and then publish or update

84
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:49,000
 the translated content back to Contentful.

85
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,000
 And this is a lot of manual work.

86
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:56,000
 However, with MCP, you can automate the entire workflow

87
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:01,000
 and do all of this work inside the LLM conversation itself.

88
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:04,000
 So basically you can do the entire workflow

89
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:06,000
 inside Cloud Disktop.

90
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:12,000
 With MCP, you can ask the LLM to translate the latest blog post

91
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:13,000
 into German.

92
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:18,000
 Again, LLM will check for a Contentful MCP server integration.

93
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:23,000
 If there is one, it will send the request to that MCP server.

94
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:27,000
 The Contentful MCP server, first, it will authenticate

95
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:30,000
 to Contentful using your token.

96
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:34,000
 Second, it will fetch your blog post content

97
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:37,000
 and then it will return it back to the LLM.

98
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:40,000
 At this point, the LLM will translate the text

99
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,000
 and here is the powerful part.

100
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:46,000
 It can ask you if you are satisfied with that translation.

101
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:50,000
 And if this is the case, if you are satisfied with the translation,

102
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:55,000
 then again, the LLM can publish the translated content back

103
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:59,000
 into Contentful using the same MCP server.

104
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:02,000
 Now, at this point, you have a German version

105
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:07,000
 of your website content automatically created and published.

106
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:11,000
 I brought this example to highlight that the MCP server

107
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:16,000
 can provide both read and write access to your data,

108
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
 which is very, very powerful.

109
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:23,000
 This was a very high level overview of how MCP works.

110
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,000
 In my opinion, MCP is a game changer

111
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:32,000
 because it transforms the LLM from isolated text generator

112
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:37,000
 into powerful assistance that can access and modify your data.

113
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,000
 And that opens the door to a new vulnerabilities

114
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:42,000
 and a new attack vectors.

115
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:45,000
 In the next lectures, we will dive deeper

116
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:48,000
 into MCP architecture and implementation

117
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:52,000
 and we will start building a local MCP server.

118
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:55,000
 I hope this picture has been informative for you

119
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:57,420
 and I would love to see you in the next lecture.

120
00:06:57,420 --> 00:06:58,620
 Thanks for watching.

